/*  A simplified command shell, read-eval-loop over standard input.

    Six permitted commands (clear, pwd, time, date, ls, which), arguments passed
    through, anything else refused. `Quit!!!` on a line by itself exits; so does
    end of input.

    Two things worth noticing:

    - There is no shell underneath this shell. `run` is fork + execvp directly —
        the program name and each argument cross to the child as separate OS-level
        arguments, so nothing is ever re-parsed by /bin/sh and quoting cannot bite.

    - `run` takes its arguments as a fixed list, so the dispatch below branches on
        how many words were typed rather than building an array. That is the whole
        shape of the program: split the line, look at what you got, choose a call.

    Run it interpreted, or compile it — `cufet build examples/shell.cufe` produces
    a native binary with no managed runtime under it. The subprocess machinery is
    POSIX, so a compiled shell needs Linux, macOS, or WSL. */

Bind fact to permitted, given (the text name):
    If name is "clear", return true.
    If name is "pwd",   return true.
    If name is "time",  return true.
    If name is "date",  return true.
    If name is "ls",    return true.
    If name is "which", return true.
    Return false.
Done.

/* A child's stderr is worth seeing before its stdout — an error explains an empty
   result. Both arrive already captured, so this is the only place either is shown. */
Bind void to show, given (the text out, the text err):
    If the length of err is greater than 0, state err trimmed.
    If the length of out is greater than 0, state out trimmed.
Done.

Define running as true.
While running, repeat:
    Define raw as read a line from the input.

    /* Void means end of input — the user closed the stream instead of saying Quit!!! */
    If raw is void, the running becomes false.
    If raw is not void:
        Define line as raw trimmed.

        If line is "Quit!!!", running becomes false.
        If line is not "Quit!!!":
            /* A blank line is not an error; it is just nothing to do. */
            If the length of line is greater than 0:
                Define parts as line split by " ".
                Define cmd   as the first of parts.
                Define n     as the number of parts.

                /* `cd` is the shell's own, not a program to launch. A child process cannot change
                   its parent's directory, which is why every real shell builds this one in. With
                   it, the permitted `pwd` finally reports somewhere you chose. A bad path is a
                   failure value, so a typo costs you a line of output and not the session. */
                If cmd is "cd":
                    Try to:
                        If n is 1:
                            Define home as the environment variable "HOME" but void is "".
                            If the length of home is 0:
                                home becomes the environment variable "USERPROFILE" but void is "/".
                            Done.
                            The current directory becomes home.
                        Done.
                        If n is 2, the current directory becomes item 2 of parts.
                        If n is greater than 2, state "cufetsh: cd: too many arguments".
                    Done.
                    In case of failure:
                        State "cufetsh: cd: {the message of the failure}".
                    Done.
                Done.

                If cast permitted on (cmd):
                    /* Launching can fail — the program may not exist, or may not be
                       executable. That is a failure value, not a crash, so it is
                       handled here and the loop carries on. */
                    Try to:
                        If n is 1:
                            Define r as run cmd.
                            Cast show on (the output of r, the errors of r).
                        Done.
                        If n is 2:
                            Define r2 as run cmd with arguments (item 2 of parts).
                            Cast show on (the output of r2, the errors of r2).
                        Done.
                        If n is 3:
                            Define r3 as run cmd with arguments (item 2 of parts, item 3 of parts).
                            Cast show on (the output of r3, the errors of r3).
                        Done.
                        If n is greater than 3, state "cufetsh: too many arguments".
                    Done.
                    In case of failure:
                        State "cufetsh: {the message of the failure}".
                    Done.
                Done.

                /* 'cd' is handled above and is not a program, so it must not fall through to the
                   refusal — it is not permitted to LAUNCH, which is a different thing. */
                If cast permitted on (cmd) is false and cmd is not "cd", state "cufetsh: {cmd}: command not permitted".
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
